WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.090
YUFENG GUO: Are you looking
to improve the reproducibility

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.270
and reliability of your data
science and machine learning

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.490
workflows and training jobs?

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.494
Then you've got try out
AI Platform Pipelines.

00:00:12.494 --> 00:00:15.938
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.400
Welcome to AI Adventures where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.470
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.930
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.340
we're going to explore how to
set up AI Platform Pipelines

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.340
to address your MLOps needs.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.980
MLOps is all the
rage these days--

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.190
a combination of machine
learning and operations.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.430
It applies many
DevOps best practices

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.740
to the machine
learning ecosystem.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.450
We often hear
about how important

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.120
it is to have a well-running
continuous integration

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.315
and deployment pipeline
for software development.

00:00:55.315 --> 00:00:58.210
So as your machine
learning setup matures,

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.730
you should begin to incorporate
these best practices as well.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.050
AI Platform Pipelines allows
you to orchestrate your machine

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.680
learning workflows as
reproducible and reusable

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.700
pipelines.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.590
Specifically, it
handles the process

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:16.120
of setting up Kubeflow Pipelines
with TensorFlow Extended

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.760
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.120
Let's talk a little more
about each of these terms.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.730
Kubeflow is an
open-source toolkit

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:29.050
for running machine learning
workflows on Kubernetes.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.320
Kubeflow Pipelines is
a component of Kubeflow

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.170
that provides a platform
for building and deploying

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.320
ML workflows, called
pipelines, structured

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.170
as directed acyclic graphs.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.230
TensorFlow Extended, or TFX,
is also an open-source project,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.160
which you can use to define
your TensorFlow-based machine

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.540
learning workflows
as a pipeline.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.600
TFX provides a
Pipeline Template that

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.690
includes components you can
reuse to ingest and transform

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.090
data, train and
evaluate a model,

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.000
deploy a train model for
inference, and so on.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.330
By reusing TFX components, you
can orchestrate your machine

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.400
learning process
without the need

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.669
to build custom components
for every single step.

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:12.160
Setting up AI Platform
Pipelines is quite easy.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.050
The team has worked hard
to make this step as

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.600
simple as possible,
especially for those

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.230
who are not familiar with
Kubernetes or DevOps.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.140
There's a dedicated dashboard
for our pipelines in the AI

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.180
Platform menu in Cloud Console.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.270
From there, we can
deploy a new pipeline.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.010
This button will
actually take us

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.100
to the Cloud Marketplace,
which is the same place we get

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.230
our deep learning VM images.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:38.080
After we click Launch, we are
presented with a configuration

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.280
page which has two sections.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.600
First, we need to either
create a Kubernetes cluster

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.490
or select an existing cluster.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.580
Notice that this
is zone-specific.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:49.960
If you're creating
a new cluster,

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.730
be sure to check the box to
allow access to cloud APIs.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:58.000
This action grants the
cluster access to your project

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.430
and saves you the effort
of creating and managing

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.850
a service account or
creating a Kubernetes secret.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.970
Once that's all set,
click Create Cluster

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.970
and then wait while
the cluster is created.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.080
During this time, the necessary
VMs will be provisioned,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.160
the Kubernetes cluster
will be created,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.310
and the Kubernetes
software is installed.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.320
What I really like
about this process

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.920
is that Kubernetes has been
abstracted away and automated.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.440
Once your cluster is
created, all we need to do

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.160
is give our pipelines instance
a name and click Deploy.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.470
Now, we wait some more
while the Kubeflow pipeline

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.360
software is installed.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.090
Once Pipelines is
deployed to your cluster,

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:40.180
you end up mostly working in
the Pipelines Dashboard UI.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.390
The Pipelines Dashboard uses
a left-hand navigation bar

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.190
with a number of
different categories,

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.870
beginning with
Getting Started which

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.170
contains a handful of
useful demos and tutorials

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:54.130
with examples of both TFX
and Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.930
Next up is the Pipelines Menu--

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.750
the main event and
the reason we're here.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.970
We can see a list of
all our pipelines.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.360
The setup process deployed a
few demo and tutorial pipelines

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.420
to the cluster so you can
get started right away.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.940
Let's start by running
the data passing pipeline

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:13.030
by clicking on its name
and the Create Run button.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.950
We can include it
in an experiment

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.010
or just leave the field
blank to associate

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:21.010
it with the default experiment
which acts as a catchall.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.900
Notice that this pipeline
has no parameters,

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.590
so we can click Start without
entering any additional fields.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.080
Now we have a pipeline running.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.170
Notice the dashboard is now
showing us the list of runs

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.820
in the Experiments tab.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.490
Let's click on our run
and see it in progress.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.910
Notice the graph will animate
each component in the pipeline

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.290
as it runs.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.300
After a little while,
the job is finished.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.840
And the green check
replaces that timer icon.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.120
Pipelines are like templates.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.030
They define what needs to
be run but don't represent

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.340
the actual execution of code.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:55.870
That's what runs are for.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.560
You must run a pipeline in
order to get any output.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.680
Each time you run a pipeline,
a new set of outputs

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:03.790
are produced.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.480
These runs are single
executions of a pipeline.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.460
And individual runs
are associated together

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.140
into an experiment.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.270
You can name your experiments
whatever you want.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.770
And you're not
constrained to having

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:17.930
the same type of pipeline
runs in one experiment.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.660
So you can include
different types

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:23.950
of pipelines in the same
experiment if you want to.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.650
Running pipelines
can output artifacts.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:28.690
These artifacts are
viewed by drilling down

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.930
into an individual run, or
by clicking the Artifacts

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.720
tab in the left-hand menu.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.030
Kubeflow Pipelines supports
the following output viewers

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:41.890
currently-- confusion matrix,
markdown, ROC curve, table,

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.530
TensorBoard, and even
a web app if you really

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.060
want to get fancy.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:48.370
Now you might be
thinking, this pipelines

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.410
thing sounds really
awesome, but how

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.630
do I create my own pipeline?

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.910
At the end of the day, we
have two different ways

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.500
to make pipelines.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.370
AI Platform Pipelines
supports both the TFX SDK

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.190
and the Kubeflow Pipelines SDK.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.210
How do we choose
which one to use?

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.470
Well, if you're working
with TensorFlow, use TFX.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:11.470
It's designed for TensorFlow and
will make your TensorFlow code

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.150
run seamlessly.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:15.610
Otherwise, use
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.020
It's designed for any
arbitrary library.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.470
So you can run everything
from your scikit-learn models

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.040
to Py Torch in your pipeline.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.090
TFX has the advantage of
more pre-built components

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.050
and is a more
opinionated approach,

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.550
while Kubeflow Pipelines
is more open-ended

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.380
and allows you to
design your own pipeline

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.625
components from scratch.

00:06:34.625 --> 00:06:36.250
We'll be looking at
more details on how

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.970
to use these two SDKs in future
episodes of AI Adventures.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.330
But for now, I've linked to
two longer videos exploring

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:46.270
AI Platform Pipelines in more
detail in the description

